## Getting Started

### Prerequisites

Install the following:

- Python 3.11 or later
- PostgreSQL
- Git
- Visual Studio Code or another Python IDE

### 1. Clone the Repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/UGDDulmina/AI-Powered-Customer-Support-Ticket-Automation

Navigate to the project directory:

```bash
cd ai-support-backend
```

### 2. Create a Virtual Environment

```bash
python -m venv .venv
```

Activate the virtual environment.

Windows:

```bash
.venv\Scripts\activate
```

Linux/macOS:

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
```

### 3. Install Dependencies

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 4. Configure PostgreSQL

Create a PostgreSQL database:

```sql
CREATE DATABASE ai_support_db;
```

### 5. Configure Environment Variables

Create a `.env` file in the project root.

Example:

```env
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres:YOUR_PASSWORD@localhost:5432/ai_support_db
```

Adjust the database connection string according to the PostgreSQL driver and configuration used in your application.

Never commit real database credentials or the `.env` file to GitHub.

### 6. Run Database Migrations

```bash
alembic upgrade head
```

This applies the available Alembic migrations to your database.

### 7. Start the FastAPI Server

```bash
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

The application should be available at:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

### 8. Open API Documentation

Swagger UI:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs
```

ReDoc:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8000/redoc
```

## Database Design

The application uses PostgreSQL with SQLAlchemy ORM.

### Main Entities

Customer

Stores customer information.

Ticket

Stores support requests, status, priority, category, and assignment information.

TicketMessage

Stores conversations associated with support tickets.

SupportAgent

Stores support agent information and active status.

TicketActivity

Stores audit history for ticket-related changes.

### Relationships

- One customer can create multiple tickets.
- One ticket belongs to one customer.
- One ticket can contain multiple messages.
- One support agent can be assigned multiple tickets.
- One ticket can have one currently assigned agent.
- One ticket can have multiple activity records.

## Error Handling

The application uses HTTP status codes to indicate API outcomes.



| 200 | Successful request 
| 201 | Resource created 
| 404 | Resource not found 
| 409 | Conflict or invalid workflow transition 
| 422 | Request validation error 
| 500 | Internal server error 

## Development and Testing

The project uses FastAPI's interactive Swagger UI for manual endpoint testing.

Important test scenarios include:

- Creating customers and tickets.
- Updating ticket status and priority.
- Validating ticket status transitions.
- Assigning and reassigning agents.
- Rejecting inactive agents.
- Filtering and paginating tickets.
- Recording ticket activities.
- Preventing duplicate activity records.
- Retrieving chronological activity history.
- Verifying database transaction consistency.

Automated tests and full integration verification remain part of the development roadmap.

